\title{A Fixed-Point Bridge for Intersecting Families of Permutations and a Large Extremal $t$-Intersecting Family}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the elementary combinatorial infrastructure behind the theory of intersecting families of permutations---the permutation analogue of the Erd\H{o}s--Ko--Rado and Complete Intersection theorems of extremal set theory. Two permutations of an $n$-element set \emph{agree} at a position when they send it to the same value; a family of permutations is \emph{intersecting} if every pair agrees somewhere, and $t$-\emph{intersecting} if every pair agrees in at least $t$ positions. Our central structural observation is the \emph{fixed-point bridge}: the set of positions on which two permutations $\sigma$ and $\tau$ agree is exactly the set of fixed points of the quotient permutation $\sigma^{-1}\tau$. Consequently a family is intersecting if and only if none of its pairwise quotients is a derangement, and the number of agreements between $\sigma$ and $\tau$ equals $n$ minus the size of the support of $\sigma^{-1}\tau$. Using this reformulation we give a fully constructive proof of the lower-bound half of the permutation Complete Intersection Theorem: for every $t$ and $m$, the \emph{prefix stabilizer}---the family of all permutations of $t+m$ points fixing each of the first $t$ points---is $t$-intersecting and has exactly $m! = (n-t)!$ members. The count is obtained by identifying the prefix stabilizer with the group of permutations preserving a coloring whose classes are $t$ singletons and one block of size $m$, whose cardinality is the product of the factorials of the class sizes. We discuss algorithms, numerical illustrations, and directions toward the matching upper bound and its variants.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Extremal set theory begins with a deceptively simple question: how large can a family of $k$-element subsets of an $n$-set be if every two of them intersect? The Erd\H{o}s--Ko--Rado theorem answers it, and the Complete Intersection Theorem of Ahlswede and Khachatrian gives the sharp answer to the $t$-wise variant, in which every two sets must share at least $t$ elements. Replacing subsets with permutations produces an equally natural and, in several respects, richer theory. Here the objects are the bijections of a finite set, two of them ``intersect'' when they agree in a coordinate, and the extremal question---first studied by Deza and Frankl in 1977 and sharpened by Kupavskii and collaborators---asks for the largest family in which every pair agrees.

The purpose of this paper is twofold. First, we isolate and prove a small structural principle, the \emph{fixed-point bridge}, that converts every statement about coordinate-agreement of permutations into a statement about the fixed points (equivalently, the support) of a single derived permutation. This dissolves the set-system bookkeeping that clutters the classical formulations. Second, we use it to give a clean, constructive proof of the lower-bound half of the permutation Complete Intersection Theorem: a $t$-intersecting family of permutations of $n = t+m$ points of size exactly $(n-t)! = m!$ exists, realized by the prefix stabilizer.

Throughout, we work with permutations of the set $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$, written $\Sym(n)$, using standard function composition; the results are phrased in a form invariant under the choice of composition convention.

\section{Definitions}

Let $n$ be a nonnegative integer and let $\Sym(n)$ denote the group of all permutations (bijections) of $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Agreement set]
For permutations $\sigma, \tau \in \Sym(n)$, the \emph{agreement set} is
\[
\Agr(\sigma, \tau) = \{\, i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\} : \sigma(i) = \tau(i) \,\}.
\]
Its cardinality $|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)|$ is the number of positions at which $\sigma$ and $\tau$ agree.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Support and derangement]
The \emph{support} of a permutation $\pi$ is $\supp(\pi) = \{\, i : \pi(i) \neq i \,\}$, the set of points it moves. A permutation is a \emph{derangement} if $\supp(\pi) = \{0,\dots,n-1\}$, i.e. it has no fixed point.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Intersecting and $t$-intersecting families]
A family $F \subseteq \Sym(n)$ is \emph{intersecting} if $\Agr(\sigma,\tau) \neq \varnothing$ for all $\sigma, \tau \in F$. For an integer $t \geq 0$, it is $t$-\emph{intersecting} if $|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)| \geq t$ for all $\sigma, \tau \in F$. (Thus ``intersecting'' is ``$1$-intersecting''.)
\end{definition}

The intersecting condition is imposed on all ordered pairs, including $\sigma = \tau$; the diagonal case is automatic since $\Agr(\sigma,\sigma) = \{0,\dots,n-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Prefix stabilizer]
For $t, m \geq 0$ and $n = t+m$, the \emph{prefix stabilizer} $\Fix_t \subseteq \Sym(n)$ is the set of all permutations fixing each of the first $t$ points:
\[
\Fix_t = \{\, \sigma \in \Sym(t+m) : \sigma(i) = i \text{ for all } i < t \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The fixed-point bridge}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed-point bridge, pointwise]\label{lem:pointwise}
For all $\sigma, \tau \in \Sym(n)$ and every position $i$,
\[
\sigma(i) = \tau(i) \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad (\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i) = i.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $\sigma(i) = \tau(i)$, apply $\sigma^{-1}$ to both sides: $i = \sigma^{-1}(\sigma(i)) = \sigma^{-1}(\tau(i)) = (\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i)$. Conversely, if $(\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i) = i$, apply $\sigma$: $\sigma(i) = \sigma((\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i)) = \tau(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point bridge, as sets]\label{thm:sets}
For all $\sigma, \tau \in \Sym(n)$,
\[
\Agr(\sigma,\tau) = \{\, i : (\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i) = i \,\} = \Fix(\sigma^{-1}\tau),
\]
the fixed-point set of the quotient $\sigma^{-1}\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An element belongs to $\Agr(\sigma,\tau)$ iff $\sigma(i) = \tau(i)$, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:pointwise} holds iff $(\sigma^{-1}\tau)(i) = i$, i.e. iff $i$ is a fixed point of $\sigma^{-1}\tau$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Agreement count]\label{cor:count}
For all $\sigma, \tau \in \Sym(n)$,
\[
|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)| = n - \big|\supp(\sigma^{-1}\tau)\big|.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The fixed-point set of any $\pi \in \Sym(n)$ is the complement of its support, so $|\Fix(\pi)| = n - |\supp(\pi)|$. Apply this to $\pi = \sigma^{-1}\tau$ and use Theorem~\ref{thm:sets}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Intersecting $\Leftrightarrow$ no derangement quotient]\label{thm:noderange}
A family $F \subseteq \Sym(n)$ is intersecting if and only if for every $\sigma, \tau \in F$ the quotient $\sigma^{-1}\tau$ has a fixed point; equivalently, if and only if no pairwise quotient is a derangement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$F$ is intersecting iff for all $\sigma,\tau \in F$ we have $\Agr(\sigma,\tau) \neq \varnothing$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sets} this is equivalent to $\Fix(\sigma^{-1}\tau) \neq \varnothing$, i.e. to the existence of a fixed point of $\sigma^{-1}\tau$, i.e. to $\sigma^{-1}\tau$ not being a derangement.
\end{proof}

Combining Corollary~\ref{cor:count} with the definitions gives a uniform restatement of $t$-intersection: $F$ is $t$-intersecting iff $|\supp(\sigma^{-1}\tau)| \leq n - t$ for every pair.

\begin{remark}[Robustness and non-vacuity]
The bridge requires no size, uniformity, or nonemptiness hypotheses on $F$, and remains meaningful when $n = 0$, where $\Sym(0)$ is trivial. The intersecting property is phrased through nonemptiness of a concrete finite set rather than a bare existential, so it does not degenerate.
\end{remark}

\section{A large extremal $t$-intersecting family}

Fix $t, m \geq 0$ and write $n = t + m$.

\subsection{The prefix stabilizer is $t$-intersecting}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:tint}
The prefix stabilizer $\Fix_t$ is $t$-intersecting.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\sigma, \tau \in \Fix_t$. For every $i < t$ we have $\sigma(i) = i$ and $\tau(i) = i$, hence $\sigma(i) = \tau(i)$, so each of the $t$ positions $0, 1, \dots, t-1$ lies in $\Agr(\sigma,\tau)$. Therefore $|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)| \geq t$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Counting the prefix stabilizer}

\begin{definition}[Collapse map]
Define $c : \{0,\dots,t+m-1\} \to \{0,\dots,t\}$ by
\[
c(i) = \begin{cases} i, & i < t, \\ t, & i \geq t. \end{cases}
\]
Thus $c$ assigns to each of the first $t$ points its own distinct color $0,1,\dots,t-1$, and paints all remaining $m$ points with the single shared color $t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Prefix stabilizer $=$ coloring stabilizer]\label{lem:collapse}
A permutation $\sigma \in \Sym(t+m)$ fixes each of the first $t$ points if and only if it preserves the coloring $c$, i.e. $c(\sigma(i)) = c(i)$ for all $i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $\sigma(i) = i$ for all $i < t$. For $i < t$, $c(\sigma(i)) = c(i)$ trivially. For $i \geq t$, note $\sigma(i) \geq t$: otherwise $\sigma(i) = j$ for some $j < t$, but $\sigma(j) = j$ already and $\sigma$ is injective, forcing $i = j < t$, a contradiction. Hence $c(\sigma(i)) = t = c(i)$.

($\Leftarrow$) Suppose $\sigma$ preserves $c$. For $i < t$, $c(\sigma(i)) = c(i) = i$. Since the color $i < t$ is attained by the unique point $i$, this forces $\sigma(i) = i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality of the prefix stabilizer]\label{thm:card}
$|\Fix_t| = m! = (n-t)!$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:collapse}, $\Fix_t$ is exactly the stabilizer of the coloring $c$ under the action of $\Sym(t+m)$ by post-composition. A permutation preserves $c$ if and only if it permutes each color class among itself; hence the stabilizer is the direct product of the symmetric groups on the color classes, and its cardinality is the product of the factorials of the class sizes. The classes of $c$ are the $t$ singletons $\{0\}, \dots, \{t-1\}$ and the single block $\{t, \dots, t+m-1\}$ of size $m$. Therefore
\[
|\Fix_t| = \Big(\prod_{k=0}^{t-1} 1!\Big)\cdot m! = m! = (n-t)!. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Large extremal $t$-intersecting family]\label{thm:main}
For every $t \geq 0$ and $m \geq 0$, on the ground set of $n = t+m$ points there exists a $t$-intersecting family of permutations of size exactly $(n-t)! = m!$, namely the prefix stabilizer $\Fix_t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:tint} and Theorem~\ref{thm:card}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deza--Frankl lower bound, $t=1$]
For every $m \geq 0$ and $n = 1 + m$, there exists an intersecting family of permutations of $\Sym(n)$ of size $m! = (n-1)!$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main} with $t = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Edge cases]
The argument is uniform in $t$ and $m$. When $m = 0$, the family collapses to $\{\id\}$, of size $0! = 1$. When $t = 0$, the coloring $c$ is constant, its stabilizer is all of $\Sym(m)$, and the count is $(t+m)! = m!$. No case analysis is needed.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

The constructive nature of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} makes every quantity computable.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Agreement count via the quotient.} Compute $|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)|$ by direct comparison or, following Corollary~\ref{cor:count}, by forming $\sigma^{-1}\tau$ and counting its fixed points. Both run in $O(n)$; their agreement realizes the fixed-point bridge computationally.
\item \textbf{Intersecting test.} Decide whether $F$ is $t$-intersecting by checking $|\Agr(\sigma,\tau)| \geq t$ over all ordered pairs, in $O(|F|^2 n)$.
\item \textbf{Prefix-stabilizer enumeration.} Generate all $m!$ permutations of the last $m$ points and extend each by the identity on the first $t$, producing exactly $(n-t)!$ certified $t$-intersecting permutations.
\end{itemize}

\section{Numerical illustrations}

\begin{itemize}
\item $t=1$, $n=3$: permutations of $\{0,1,2\}$ fixing $0$ are $\id$ and $(1\;2)$, giving $2 = 2!$.
\item $t=2$, $n=4$: permutations fixing $0$ and $1$ are $\id$ and $(2\;3)$, giving $2 = 2!$.
\item $t=3$, $n=3$: only $\id$, giving $1 = 0!$.
\item Bridge check on $\Sym(3)$: for $\sigma = (0\;1\;2)$, $\tau = (0\;2\;1)$ the quotient $\sigma^{-1}\tau$ is a derangement and $\sigma, \tau$ disagree everywhere; for $\sigma = \id$ the agreements with any $\tau$ are the fixed points of $\tau$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications}

The theory of intersecting permutations is the permutation face of the Erd\H{o}s--Ko--Rado and Complete Intersection theorems. Constraining how often two arrangements may agree is the setting of \emph{permutation codes}, where large minimum disagreement corresponds to large minimum Hamming distance, a model used for flash memory and powerline communication. Scheduling and seating problems share the same combinatorial structure. The fixed-point bridge connects the extremal theory to the classical study of derangements and to the representation theory of the symmetric group, which supplies the spectral tools for the sharpest upper bounds.

\section{Discussion}

The value of the fixed-point bridge is compression. The intersecting condition, phrased naively, is a quantifier over all pairs and all coordinates. The bridge replaces the inner coordinate quantifier with a single structural predicate---``the quotient has a fixed point''---turning an extremal set-system problem into a question about the distribution of support sizes across pairwise quotients. The extremal construction compresses to a single act: fix a prefix and let the remainder run free; the count follows from the elementary fact that permutations preserving a partition number the product of the factorials of the block sizes. The development is uniform (no degenerate cases) and constructive (the family is exhibited explicitly).

\section{Future directions}

\subsection*{The matching upper bound and uniqueness}
The conjecture is that, once $n$ is large relative to $t$, no $t$-intersecting family exceeds $(n-t)!$, and the prefix stabilizer is the unique extremal family up to relabelling. Since agreement equals the number of fixed points of $\sigma^{-1}\tau$, extremality should be forced by how few large-fixed-point elements the symmetric group contains; the remaining work is a spectral/counting estimate.

\subsection*{A derangement-weighted density threshold}
Replace the all-or-nothing condition with a weighted one: seek the largest family whose average pairwise disagreement stays below $\theta\cdot n$. Average disagreement is the average support size of $\sigma^{-1}\tau$, so the problem becomes optimization over distributions on the symmetric group whose typical element has small support.

\subsection*{Cross-intersecting pairs}
For families $A, B$ with every member of $A$ agreeing in $\geq t$ positions with every member of $B$, maximize $|A|\cdot|B|$. Conjecture: for large $n$ the optimum is $((n-t)!)^2$, attained by $A = B$ a common prefix stabilizer.

\subsection*{Fixed-point spectra and forbidden intersection sizes}
Characterize which sets of agreement-counts can occur among pairwise quotients, and study families forbidding a prescribed intersection size---the permutation analogue of forbidden-intersection problems.

\section{Conclusion}

We reduced the coordinate-agreement structure of permutations to the fixed points of a single derived permutation and used this fixed-point bridge to prove, uniformly and constructively, that the prefix stabilizer is a $t$-intersecting family of exactly $(n-t)!$ permutations---the lower-bound half of the permutation Complete Intersection Theorem, with the Deza--Frankl $(n-1)!$ bound as the case $t=1$.

